  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1024 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA chilly Thanksgiving morning with lows generally in the 20s \nacross the plains will give way to above normal temperatures in \nthe afternoon. Highs for Thanksgiving will be in the 50s to near \n60 for the plains and 30s to mid 40s for the mountains and \nvalleys. Winds will remain on the lighter side with mostly sunny \nto partly cloudy skies for the holiday.\n\nFor the system this weekend, the GFS has trended closer to what \nthe Euro (and a few other models) has been showing over the past \nfew days. The increased model consensus has allowed our forecast \nconfidence to increase for this weekend. Upper level northwest \nflow will strengthen on Friday, bringing breezy conditions to the \nhigher terrain starting Friday morning. Wind gusts will be around \n40 to 65mph on Friday for elevations generally above 9000ft. We \ncould see some stronger winds come off of the Cheyenne ridge and \ninto our northern plains counties Friday afternoon as well, with \ngusts around 25 to 40mph. Downsloping northwest winds will allow \ntemperatures to warm further, putting highs around 60 degrees for \nmuch of the plains on Friday.\n\nAn upper level shortwave will approach the area late Friday, moving \nthrough by late Saturday. Ahead of the shortwave, we'll see \nincreasing moisture and decent QG lift. An upper level jet is also \nforecast to develop, with our area in the left exit region for \nmuch of Saturday. At the surface, a strong cold front will move \nthrough Saturday afternoon, bringing strong winds (gusting around \n30 to 55mph) and much cooler temperatures. A few models are showing\nSBCAPE above 50J/kg on Saturday, which combined with the strong \nfrontogenesis is causing some concern for banded snowfall and \npotentially even snow squalls. This and the potential for blowing\nsnow from the strong winds could lead to hazardous travel\nconditions Saturday. The other thing we'll need to watch is the \npotential for anticyclonic turning of the winds as it comes off \nthe Cheyenne Ridge. A slight eastward turning would increase the \npotential for higher snow amounts in the southern foothills, south\nmetro, and Palmer Divide area. \n\nThe highest snow accumulations are forecast to be in the Park\nRange with most likely liquid totals (25th to 75th percentiles) \nof 0.6 to 1.6 inches. With SLRs between 10 and 15, a Winter\nWeather Advisory is likely once we get closer, with potential for\na Winter Storm Warning. The rest of our mountain areas are\ncurrently forecast to see between 2 and 8 inches of snow with \nthis event. For the plains, precipitation will generally be light \nwith rain transitioning to snow behind the front. Snow \naccumulations are forecast to generally remain below 0.5 inches \nfor the urban corridor and plains, with higher amounts for the \nPalmer and Foothills. \n\nThis system will move off to the east late Saturday, leaving \ncolder and drier conditions for Sunday. Lows Sunday morning in the\nplains will be in the teens to 20s and highs will be in the 40s. \nWinds will remain breezy Sunday morning, putting apparent \ntemperatures in the low teens to single digits for the eastern \nplains. An active pattern will continue through the middle of next\nweek. We could see a few more chances for mountain snow next week as\nan upper level shortwave moves through Monday and our next bigger\nsystem approaches Wednesday.",
